            "text": "{\"study_topic_id\":\"study-0c2663d75760b032fac9\",\"overview\":{\"text\":\"A square root answers the question, “What number multiplied by itself gives this value?” For a nonnegative real number a, its principal square root, written √a, is the nonnegative r such that r² = a; for instance, √25 = 5. This convention makes the radical symbol single-valued, even though 25 has two square roots, 5 and −5. In an equation like x² = 25, both signs are solutions, often written x = ±5. Square roots reverse squaring and are useful for recovering a square’s side length from its area. The same operation can be written as raising a nonnegative number to the power 1/2.\",\"source_urls\":[\"https://openstax.org/books/college-algebra-2e/pages/1-3-radicals-and-rational-exponents\",\"https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra/pages/5-7-simplify-and-use-square-roots\",\"https://www.mathsisfun.com/algebra/square-root.html\"]},\"key_facts\":[{\"text\":\"The square-root symbol √ is called a radical; the number beneath it is the radicand.\",\"source_urls\":[\"https://openstax.org/books/college-algebra-2e/pages/1-3-radicals-and-rational-exponents\"]},{\"text\":\"For a positive number, the two square roots are opposites; the radical symbol denotes the nonnegative, principal one. Zero has just one square root: 0.\",\"source_urls\":[\"https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra/pages/5-7-simplify-and-use-square-roots\",\"https://www.mathsisfun.com/algebra/square-root.html\"]},{\"text\":\"Solving x² = a for positive a gives x = √a or x = −√a, often written x = ±√a; this is different from the single value of √a.\",\"source_urls\":[\"https://www.mathsisfun.com/algebra/square-root.html\",\"https://openstax.org/books/elementary-algebra/pages/10-1-solve-quadratic-equations-using-the-square-root-property\"]},{\"text\":\"For nonnegative a, the square root can also be written a^(1/2).\",\"source_urls\":[\"https://www.mathsisfun.com/algebra/square-root.html\",\"https://openstax.org/books/college-algebra-2e/pages/1-3-radicals-and-rational-exponents\"]},{\"text\":\"If a square’s area is A, its side length is √A, so square roots can help reverse an area calculation.\",\"source_urls\":[\"https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra/pages/5-7-simplify-and-use-square-roots\"]}],\"sources\":[{\"title\":\"1.3 Radicals and Rational Exponents - College Algebra 2e\",\"url\":\"https://openstax.org/books/college-algebra-2e/pages/1-3-radicals-and-rational-exponents\",\"publisher\":\"OpenStax\",\"retrieved_at\":\"2026-09-24T18:42:02.600060+00:00\",\"supports\":\"Definition of the principal square root, radical and radicand terminology, examples, and equivalence of rational exponents and roots.\"},{\"title\":\"5.7 Simplify and Use Square Roots - Prealgebra\",\"url\":\"https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra/pages/5-7-simplify-and-use-square-roots\",\"publisher\":\"OpenStax\",\"retrieved_at\":\"2026-09-24T18:42:02.600060+00:00\",\"supports\":\"Square roots as values whose squares give the radicand; positive and negative roots, the principal-root convention, zero, and finding a square’s side from its area.\"},{\"title\":\"Squares and Square Roots in Algebra\",\"url\":\"https://www.mathsisfun.com/algebra/square-root.html\",\"publisher\":\"Math Is Fun\",\"retrieved_at\":\"2026-09-24T18:42:02.600060+00:00\",\"supports\":\"Square roots as reversing squaring; opposite roots, the principal-root notation, solving squared equations with ±, and the one-half exponent.\"},{\"title\":\"10.1 Solve Quadratic Equations Using the Square Root Property - Elementary Algebra\",\"url\":\"https://openstax.org/books/elementary-algebra/pages/10-1-solve-quadratic-equations-using-the-square-root-property\",\"publisher\":\"OpenStax\",\"retrieved_at\":\"2026-09-24T18:42:02.600060+00:00\",\"supports\":\"The square-root property gives two solutions, the principal square root and its opposite, for equations of the form x² = k when k is nonnegative.\"}],\"research_queries\":[\"site:khanacademy.org square roots principal square root definition\",\"site:openstax.org square root principal square root nonnegative\",\"site:mathsisfun.com square root definition principal square root\"],\"notes\":\"The overview is one original paragraph within the requested word range. The distinction between the principal value √a and the two solutions of x² = a is explicit.\",\"unresolved_concerns\":[],\"self_check_complete\":true}"
